\section{Answer-Conditioned Reasoning + Distillation (ACRD, SFT)}

ACRD augments the standard RLVR loop with a per-batch reasoning trace generator
that is conditioned on the ground-truth label and then distilled via SFT. The
goal is to teach the model how to justify correct labels without exposing the
answer at inference time.

\subsection*{Setup}
Each training sample is $(x, y^\*)$, where $x$ is the original task prompt and
$y^\*$ is the ground-truth label (single ID, label set, or CVSS vector depending
on the task). We train with the same RLVR rollout policy and reward used for
Minerva tasks.

\subsection*{Step 1: RLVR Rollouts}
For each batch, the actor generates $n$ RLVR responses on the original prompt
$x$ (no hints). The reward $r_{\text{base}} \in [0,1]$ is computed with the
standard Minerva verifier.

\subsection*{Step 2: Answer-Conditioned Reasoning (ACR) Generation}
We build an ACR prompt by appending an answer-conditioned block to the last user
message of $x$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GROUND\_TRUTH\_LABELS}: the gold label(s) $y^\*$.
  \item \textbf{LABEL\_REFERENCE}: canonical label details (if available).
  \item \textbf{Instructions}: produce a short reasoning and end with the final
    answer in the original task format.
\end{itemize}
The label details are drawn from a canonical store (per entity type) and
truncated as needed to respect a maximum prompt length. If the prompt remains
too long after truncation, ACR is skipped for that sample.

We only generate ACR traces for hard examples, using the RLVR rollouts from the
same batch. Let $\hat{r}_{\max}$ be the maximum RLVR reward for a UID; ACR is
run only if:
\[
\hat{r}_{\max} < 1.0.
\]
For eligible samples we generate $k$ ACR rollouts with the same actor.

\subsection*{Step 3: ACR Reward and Filtering}
Each ACR response is scored with the same extraction logic as RLVR to obtain the
base correctness score $r_{\text{base}}$. We then apply leakage guards (banned
phrases, minimum reasoning length, minimum overlap with task context, and
verbatim label-detail overlap). The scalar ACR score is:
\[
r_{\text{acr}} = r_{\text{correct}} \cdot r_{\text{base}} - \lambda \cdot
\mathbb{1}[\text{leak}],
\]
where $r_{\text{correct}}$ and $\lambda$ are fixed weights. ACR traces are
eligible for distillation if:
\[
r_{\text{base}} \ge \tau \quad \wedge \quad \text{leak} = \text{false}.
\]
We also drop degenerate traces with high n-gram repetition.

\subsection*{Step 4: Per-batch SFT Distillation}
For each UID, we select one eligible ACR trace (uniform random by default) and
store it in a rolling buffer. Every $I$ RLVR steps, we sample a minibatch of
accepted traces and perform an SFT update using the \emph{original} prompt $x$
(no hints) and the ACR response $y_{\text{acr}}$:
\[
\mathcal{L}_{\text{sft}} = -\sum_{t} \log p_\theta(y_{\text{acr},t} \mid x).
\]
This distillation step teaches the model to internalize the reasoning and final
answer format without exposing ground-truth labels at inference time.

\subsection*{Implementation Notes}
ACRD is implemented as a per-batch augmentation inside the RLVR trainer. It
requires raw chat prompts to append the ACR block, and uses a label-details
store keyed by entity type (e.g., ATT\&CK technique, CWE, CAPEC). The ACR traces
are filtered before distillation to prevent leakage and low-quality reasoning
from entering the SFT buffer.
